#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128899 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "503a2d0a-ba6c-5529-9f26-b83c769a26a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "b7a64276-0aab-5ef0-861b-528ebbd66e60" :question/math-academy-id "e-5839" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2-x}+1$?" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except those that make the denominator zero. To find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 - x &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x \\in (-\\infty,2) \\cup (2,\\infty)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128900 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd0aa5fc-79de-525e-a688-a8a2d2fcf858" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant in a Transformed Reciprocal Function Given the Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "1d4c0776-f7ea-5529-bb58-8f9d892c9551" :question/math-academy-id "e-5840" :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{5}{2x-a}+3$ is $(-\\infty,3) \\cup (3,+\\infty)$. What is the value of $a$?" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - a &= 0 \\\\\n2x &= a \\\\\nx &= \\frac{a}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{a}{2}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=3$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a}{2} &= 3 \\\\\na &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128901 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d82fa3-d576-5c42-9dca-4e1b76473424" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "fb0fd8d0-a34c-509f-a13d-dc39464262c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5841" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{3}{4x-1}+2$." :question/worked-solution "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{a}{bx+c} + d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d$, and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\in (-\\infty, d)\\cup (d,\\infty)\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d = 2$. Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 2$, and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\in (-\\infty, 2) \\cup (2, \\infty)\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2082/Source/Images/936fa12fa348e9787c6996f12c05b858.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d82fa3-d576-5c42-9dca-4e1b76473424" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a73ab02-f495-5d16-bce5-a84ae29da6a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,4)\\cup (4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243261} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2ce45f-7555-50a7-8860-019c428c246f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243262} {:answer/id #uuid "0375f2dc-639b-5aed-9cfb-53f43ab8bebe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{5})\\cup (\\frac{1}{5},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243263} {:answer/id #uuid "997903fd-e21d-5148-ac8c-0613f153c73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{4})\\cup (\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243264} {:answer/id #uuid "572adc0c-f22e-5ca8-9fb0-9ae412a3fd47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{4})\\cup (-\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "997903fd-e21d-5148-ac8c-0613f153c73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{4})\\cup (\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243264} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a0797e6-bb4b-595b-af6a-99d2c52cf4d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a645941-af8f-5b77-a3ee-1cb12a563a63" :question/math-academy-id "q-106741" :question/problem "What is the range of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{1-5x}+\\frac{3}{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{3}{4},$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{4})\\cup (\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929417/assets/00f66fd1de73592b3e0113b56ee23f334c2643b1e55fc185dfa9b9126d6ad680.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd0aa5fc-79de-525e-a688-a8a2d2fcf858" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant in a Transformed Reciprocal Function Given the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45dc1dfe-8a15-547c-bb97-6d6d6a43e17d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45dc1dfe-8a15-547c-bb97-6d6d6a43e17d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243268} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd0ceac2-f057-583c-ac45-c9e60c17e6e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9521231-c2a3-5d49-b22f-f2c500b41d19" :question/math-academy-id "q-270797" :question/problem "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{6}{8x-b}$ is $(-\\infty ,5)\\cup (5,\\infty ),$ then $b=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-b & =0 \\\\ 8x & =b \\\\ x & =\\frac{b}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=\\frac{b}{8}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=5$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $\\frac{b}{8},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{8} & =5 \\\\ b & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd0aa5fc-79de-525e-a688-a8a2d2fcf858" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant in a Transformed Reciprocal Function Given the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23ee9fe3-a3ee-599f-8c30-7d88b46b1090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23ee9fe3-a3ee-599f-8c30-7d88b46b1090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243271} :answer-field/id #uuid "81c79035-1d92-5c39-a73d-d13db555cead" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4a62a31-f3c1-5d25-9577-7c2ed7a8cdeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-276641" :question/problem "If the domain of the function $q(x)=\\frac{4}{9x-k}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{3})\\cup (\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ),$ then $k=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-k & =0 \\\\ 9x & =k \\\\ x & =\\frac{k}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=\\frac{k}{9}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $\\frac{k}{9},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{9} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ k & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd0aa5fc-79de-525e-a688-a8a2d2fcf858" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant in a Transformed Reciprocal Function Given the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d289774-0946-5dae-86e6-b21d0cfd610c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d289774-0946-5dae-86e6-b21d0cfd610c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243274} :answer-field/id #uuid "c032444a-ac1a-585d-a440-96c912fd0215" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c4479f1-35eb-5eb4-98de-700106f681d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-276643" :question/problem "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{10}{kx+4}$ is $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ),$ then $k=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx+4 & =0 \\\\ kx & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=-\\frac{4}{k}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $-\\frac{4}{k},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{k} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{4}{k} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d82fa3-d576-5c42-9dca-4e1b76473424" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "887e9149-6d7a-5ecd-94cb-38f8cadc46c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "887e9149-6d7a-5ecd-94cb-38f8cadc46c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243277} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5580085-b241-546f-a5dd-6bbbe399f666" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243276} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6edfd1e3-6bfe-535b-81f1-4dbb3c3b886b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6edfd1e3-6bfe-535b-81f1-4dbb3c3b886b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243279} 