ominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "503a2d0a-ba6c-5529-9f26-b83c769a26a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3535c759-e130-5791-91c9-949efff441da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2})\\cup (\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243314} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8ded3f-6c7b-523a-b3ce-54585108afa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243315} {:answer/id #uuid "c8eb5452-2cd2-53e0-977b-ddb8ac4b8e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243316} {:answer/id #uuid "4d91205f-87f1-5e01-9f14-1dc7c73cf9a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243317} {:answer/id #uuid "647ac63b-1fa5-50cf-afa2-3a8329ee3867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2})\\cup (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d91205f-87f1-5e01-9f14-1dc7c73cf9a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243317} :answer-field/id #uuid "220c7b79-7ccf-5959-af81-106277f43df2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f952c48-2e4b-539b-af5b-7b0610b4a2cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-40017" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{2x-1}+1?$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd0aa5fc-79de-525e-a688-a8a2d2fcf858" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant in a Transformed Reciprocal Function Given the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe7331c-e62e-56ac-bd3b-2480cd9ae1cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243321} {:answer/id #uuid "252874e3-3822-5002-85e5-1a96c79f656d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243322} {:answer/id #uuid "484517dd-16e2-5d80-97eb-66603fd9fc13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243323} {:answer/id #uuid "330a2c96-9249-5d01-b95e-0d53f6d63edf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243324} {:answer/id #uuid "3590e376-8228-5784-bfdd-d521ea95e5c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fe7331c-e62e-56ac-bd3b-2480cd9ae1cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243321} :answer-field/id #uuid "eae05635-e407-5313-ab50-b214c850c745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ed2d0e2-7a11-547e-8075-2cb7de437dae" :question/math-academy-id "q-40027" :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4}{3x+b}$ is $(-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (-4,\\infty ).$ Find the value of $b.$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+b & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-b \\\\ x & =-\\frac{b}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=-\\frac{b}{3}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=-4$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $-\\frac{b}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{b}{3} & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{b}{3} & =4 \\\\ b & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "503a2d0a-ba6c-5529-9f26-b83c769a26a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2cb0c1a-1e2f-58be-933a-61779a127602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,5)\\cup (5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243328} {:answer/id #uuid "b15ccab7-28f3-583f-bea7-0d40139f58dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243329} {:answer/id #uuid "583c59b4-c3da-5d1e-8adf-d11321205077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{3})\\cup (-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243330} {:answer/id #uuid "86dcaa23-b996-5ff6-ba97-c888796f0429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{5})\\cup (\\frac{1}{5},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243331} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e0040e-5615-5c03-a5f0-15a57533efed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86dcaa23-b996-5ff6-ba97-c888796f0429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{5})\\cup (\\frac{1}{5},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243331} :answer-field/id #uuid "217677f6-a1d6-5e32-88c8-e9e750c3ec62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3aa4dd7-f3b9-50ae-800f-5341ea9434cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-81297" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{5x-1}?$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-1 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{5})\\cup (\\frac{1}{5},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd0aa5fc-79de-525e-a688-a8a2d2fcf858" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant in a Transformed Reciprocal Function Given the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f01a582-3414-5af1-b5f4-2cb99957c61e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243335} {:answer/id #uuid "48426b63-5bcc-55a7-a0ce-d8ca528d8934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243336} {:answer/id #uuid "224d48f2-74d3-548a-84dd-e4ae79386121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243337} {:answer/id #uuid "1a6735f0-e87f-5760-9636-9df9c0a9b909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243338} {:answer/id #uuid "90223d30-305f-56b8-a62a-1d2b9ccdec7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90223d30-305f-56b8-a62a-1d2b9ccdec7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243339} :answer-field/id #uuid "405a1d04-eba7-59d0-8210-5aff8f2a6e14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72eaa0c2-1bf3-5601-8b93-d0bab80d97f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-81306" :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x-7}{kx-4}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{4}{5})\\cup (\\frac{4}{5},\\infty ).$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-4 & =0 \\\\ kx & =4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=\\frac{4}{k}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $\\frac{4}{k},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{k} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{k} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d82fa3-d576-5c42-9dca-4e1b76473424" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aaaa15e-382f-5847-923a-5fb385560791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x) ∈ (- ∞, 6)∪(6, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128673} {:answer/id #uuid "bb57fdb7-bd20-5d11-89f9-0a800770a7b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x) ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128674} {:answer/id #uuid "dfcd17d7-7271-5e86-a9e4-8eca98c7f650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x) ∈ (- ∞,-6)∪(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128675} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5d1c8a-5473-50fa-8299-14d603c5fffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x) ∈ (- ∞,-1)∪(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128676} {:answer/id #uuid "5970090d-7138-5fc5-b9bc-dab3a9539bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x) ∈ (- ∞, 1)∪(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5970090d-7138-5fc5-b9bc-dab3a9539bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x) ∈ (- ∞, 1)∪(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128677} :answer-field/id #uuid "33cdf763-e2dd-5265-ab40-24b04ceb8bcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6996c90-1b1f-5226-b63d-1aa92068c6b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81416" :question/problem "What is the range of $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x - 6} + 1$?" :question/worked-solution "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=1.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1,$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929417/assets/f6b1316dd9c7f2a40c23de08dde63cbc6402593c6209d99384279a7c1ae29da6.png)"}]}
